=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is fully operational. Every component in the name-resolution and web-access chain was tested and found healthy.

DNS daemon: BIND (named) is running on dns_server (10.200.0.2) and is authoritative for the "local" zone. The zone file contains correct A records — web0.local → 10.200.0.3, web1.local → 10.200.0.4, web2.local → 10.200.0.5, web3.local → 10.200.0.6 — and named-checkconf reports no configuration errors. The `recursion no` setting is intentional for an authoritative-only server and does not affect lookups within the local zone.

Resolver on the client: host_1_1_1_1's /etc/resolv.conf correctly points to 10.200.0.2. Both nslookup and the libc stub resolver (getent hosts) return the right addresses for all four web names. Curl to http://web0.local/ completes with HTTP 200 in under 3 ms — name resolution and TCP connection both succeed.

Routing: OSPF has converged across all six FRR devices. router_core_1 holds Full adjacencies with router_core_2, router_core_3, and switch_dist_1_1. All devices carry consistent routes to 10.1.1.0/24, 10.2.1.0/24, and 10.200.0.0/24. The return path from switch_server_access back to the host subnets is also in the FIB.

Web servers: All four servers (web_server_0 – web_server_3) return HTTP 200 when accessed from host_1_1_1_1 by direct IP and by hostname. No firewall rules (iptables chains are empty/ACCEPT) on dns_server.

Root cause: There is no fault. The premise of the ticket — that DNS names fail from host_1_1_1_1 — is not reproducible; the infrastructure is healthy. No corrective action is required; the original report was likely a transient client-side issue or observer error.
=== END ===
```